Draw a **forest prop set**, a 96x96 sprite sheet of 2D scenery decorations, using
only the drawing tool.

The sheet holds eight storybook forest props laid out within one 96x96 canvas: a
round leafy tree, a pine tree, a leafy bush, a cluster of grass tufts, a mossy
boulder, a red mushroom cluster, a small flower patch, and a tree stump. Each is a
small game prop a level designer would scatter through a woodland scene — soft
shading, rounded friendly forms, and a crisp silhouette readable on full
transparency.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw` binary already on your `PATH`. It is the
sole channel: you build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not the pixels on disk. An image produced
any other way (writing a PNG directly, editing `canvas.png` by hand) is discarded
and flagged; only the recorded operations count.

After each call the binary re-renders the current image to `canvas.png` and
appends the operation to `actions.json`. A blank starting canvas and an empty log
are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
draw fill-rect --x 6 --y 40 --width 18 --height 10 --color "#6fb04a"
```

Run `draw --help` to list every operation and `draw <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`canvas.png` after your calls** to see your progress and decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the prop sheet up operation by operation until the image in `canvas.png`
matches the brief — all eight forest props, each reading clearly as itself, in the
one storybook style with soft shading and crisp silhouettes, laid out within the
frame in the named palette on full transparency with nothing clipped against an
edge. When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded `actions.json` is your finished
submission.
